diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability from Office A to the Office B server and found no fault. From PC1 (10.1.0.100) to srv1 (10.5.0.4), ICMP succeeds with 0% loss and ~2 ms RTT, and HTTP requests complete in ~1–3 ms, confirming Layer-3/Layer-7 connectivity is normal now. On the core and distribution switches, OSPF adjacencies are Full and stable: csw1 shows full neighbors on all inter-core and distro links. Routing tables on csw1/csw2 and Office A distribution (dswa1/dswa2) include 10.5.0.0/24 via the core, and Office B distribution (dswb1/dswb2) shows 10.5.0.0/24 directly connected on br0.30, aligning with the server segment. PC1 itself is correctly configured (default via 10.1.0.1).

Mechanistically, if a core link or inter-site routing had failed, PC1→10.5.0.4 pings and HTTP would fail or reroute with higher latency; we observe neither. OSPF route timers and neighbor states corroborate a healthy control plane across Office A, core, and Office B.

Root cause: No current network fault is present; the prior report cannot be reproduced and is not attributable to the core links or inter-site routing.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. If the symptom reappears intermittently, collect timestamps and check OSPF adjacency logs and interface counters on core-distro links (bond/eth5/eth6) for flaps, but at present the network is healthy.
